   "title": "Health Workers Mobilise for Showdown with FG over 13-year Salary Adjustment Delay",
   "url": "https://www.thisdaylive.com/2026/11/01/health-workers-mobilise-for-showdown-with-fg-over-13-year-salary-adjustment-delay/",
   "domain": "thisdaylive.com",
   "source": "ThisDay（尼日利亚）",
   "published": "Sun, 01 Nov 2026 02:31:05 +0000",
   "_dt": 1793500265.0,
   "ageDays": 0.77,
   "summary": "The Joint Health Sector Unions (JOHESU) have begun mobilising health workers nationwide for possible industrial action over the federal government’s failure to implement the Consolidated Health Salary Structure (CONHESS) adjustment. The union, which warned that it would resist any attempt to frustrate the salary adjustment, said it was no longer prepared to tolerate further delays in implementing the agreement.",
   "summary_zh": "联合卫生部门工会（JOHESU）已开始在全國動員醫護人員，可能因聯邦政府未能落實《綜合醫務人員薪資結構》（CONHESS）調整而發動 industrial action。該工會此前警告，將抵制任何企圖阻礙薪資調整的行為，並表示已不再準備容忍進一步延遲落實協議。",
   "summarySource": "fulltext",
   "body": "The Joint Health Sector Unions (JOHESU) have begun mobilising health workers nationwide for possible industrial action over the federal government’s failure to implement the Consolidated Health Salary Structure (CONHESS) adjustment. This demand has remained unresolved for about 13 years. The union, which warned that it would resist any attempt to frustrate the salary adjustment, said it was no longer prepared to tolerate further delays in implementing the agreement. This was part of the resolutions reached at an emergency expanded National Executive Council (NEC) virtual meeting held on Thursday, October 29, 2026, attended by its affiliates and organs at federal and state levels. The union recalled that President Bola Tinubu had, on June 5, 2023, promised to actualise the adjustment while appealing to JOHESU to suspend its strike after assuming office. It also recalled that the Federal Ministry of Health and Social Welfare had, on the same date, forwarded the 2021 High Level Body Technical Committee Report on the adjustment to the Budget Office of the Federation, the secretariat of the Presidential Committee on Salaries (PCS). However, JOHESU accused the government of allowing the process to stall because the PCS could not meet, warning that any further attempt to use the committee’s failure to act as an excuse would meet resistance. The union commended the Nigeria Labour Congress (NLC) and the Trade Union Congress of Nigeria (TUC) for taking decisive steps to secure the adjustment, describing it as the oldest demand in organised labour history. It also reminded the federal government of the provisions of the Trade Unions Act, Cap T14, LFN, 2004, which permits trade unions to negotiate benefit packages and collective bargaining agreements on behalf of their members. JOHESU consequently placed its members on alert and directed the commencement of mobilisation across health institutions nationwide for a possible industrial action and what it described as necessary but unparalleled resistance should the government yield to attempts to frustrate the demand. The union, however, urged members to remain peaceful while awaiting further directives from the NLC and TUC, following the ultimatum and timeline issued to the federal government. Founded on January 22, 1995, THISDAY is published by THISDAY NEWSPAPERS LTD., 35 Creek Road Apapa, Lagos, Nigeria with offices in 36 states of Nigeria , the Federal Capital Territory and around the world. It is Nigeria’s most authoritative news media available on all platforms for the political, business, professional and diplomatic elite and broader middle classes while serving as the meeting point of new ideas, culture and technology for the aspirationals and millennials. The newspaper is a public trust dedicated to the pursuit of truth and reason covering a range of issues from breaking news to politics, business, the markets, the arts, sports and community to the crossroads of people and society. You can email us at: hello@thisdaylive.com or visit our contact us page",
