   "title": "Keralam’s 70th anniversary marks beginning of dream journey to become India’s best State: CM",
   "url": "https://www.thehindu.com/news/national/kerala/keralams-70th-anniversary-marks-beginning-of-dream-journey-to-become-indias-best-state-cm/article71644261.ece",
   "domain": "thehindu.com",
   "source": "thehindu.com",
   "published": "Fri, 30 Oct 2026 16:47:44 +0530",
   "_dt": 1793359064.0,
   "ageDays": 2.4,
   "summary": "Unlock these with Subscription Updated - October 30, 2026 02:29 pm IST - Thiruvananthapuram The 70th anniversary of Keralam ’s formation marks the beginning of a dream journey to make the State the best in India, said Chief Minister V. Paying tributes to the countless people whose hard work helped Keralam reach where it is today, the Chief Minister said that the State must aspire to greater heights.",
   "summary_zh": "订阅即可解锁这些内容 更新于2026年10月30日 下午02:29，印度标准时间——蒂鲁文南特普勒姆 喀拉拉邦成立70周年标志着实现将该邦打造为印度最佳邦之邦梦想旅程的开始，首席部长V.表示。首席部长向无数为喀拉拉邦取得今日成就而辛勤工作的人致敬，并表示，该邦必须志在攀登更高峰。",
   "summarySource": "fulltext",
   "body": "Account subscription benefits alongside Premium Stories, Editorials, Opinions and more. Unlock these with Subscription Updated - October 30, 2026 02:29 pm IST - Thiruvananthapuram The 70th anniversary of Keralam ’s formation marks the beginning of a dream journey to make the State the best in India, said Chief Minister V. D. Satheesan, here on Friday. He was speaking at the launch of the logo for ‘Festival of Kerala 2026’ at the at the Sankara Narayanan Thampi Hall in the Legislative Assembly complex. Paying tributes to the countless people whose hard work helped Keralam reach where it is today, the Chief Minister said that the State must aspire to greater heights. He said the government was pursuing many dreams for the State and that much tireless efforts would be needed to realise these dreams. The first phase of celebrations marking seven decades of the State’s formation will be celebrated as “Festival Kerala” in Chennai, Bengaluru, Mumbai, New Delhi and Kolkata, beginning on November 1. Besides showcasing Kerala’s distinctive art forms and cultural heritage, Festival Keralam will also be an occasion to showcase the investment opportunities in the State. The government’s aim is to take Keralam to the global front ranks by achieving the country’s highest educational standards, an established health sector, industrial progress, entrepreneurship, ample employment opportunities, sound public finances, and robust welfare and development programmes. Mr. Satheesan said that while conserving Keralam’s art, literature, writing and reading traditions, the State would retain its lead in areas where it is already ahead and strive to catch up in areas where it is found lagging. He appealed for everyone’s support and cooperation in this collective endeavour. The logo of Festival of Keralam 2026 was unveiled jointly by the Chief Minister, Deputy Speaker Shanimol Usman and Minister P. C. Vishnunath, along with two representatives of the younger generation, Aaveerbhav and Eva Anil, and two 70-year-olds, Sumathi and Sundaran Nadar. Speaker Thiruvanchoor Radhakrishnan, the Deputy Speaker and Mr. Vishnunath offered felicitations. General Administration Principal Secretary B. Ashok welcomed the gathering while the Sports and Youth Affairs Secretary N. Prasanth proposed the vote of thanks. Ministers N. Shamsudheen, P. K. Basheer, Sunny Joseph, C. P. John, K. A. Thulasi, Bindu Krishna, Roji M. John, V. E. Abdul Gafoor and Mons Joseph; MLAs K. K. Rema, Praveen and Gopakumar and the Chief Secretary Biswanath Sinha were present on the occasion.’ Comments have to be in English, and in full sentences. They cannot be abusive or personal. Please abide by our community guidelines for posting your comments. We have migrated to a new commenting platform. If you are already a registered user of The Hindu and logged in, you may continue to engage with our articles. If you do not have an account please register and login to post comments. Users can access their older comments by logging into their accounts on Vuukle",
