   "title": "1/ A Chinese mining company has offered about $210 million in cash to buy Atlantic Lithium, the firm trying to open Ghana's first lithium mi",
   "url": "https://x.com/theclemeast/status/2116053796312793302",
   "snippet": "1/ A Chinese mining company has offered about $210 million in cash to buy Atlantic Lithium, the firm trying to open Ghana's first lithium mine.\nBut if you hold its shares on the GSE, there's a catch. 🧵\n\n2/ Atlantic Lithium (GSE: ALLGH) is developing the Ewoyaa lithium project in Ghana, about 100km from Accra.\n\nOn May 7, 2026, a company called Huayou agreed to buy ALL of Atlantic Lithium's shares for cash. The latest quarterly report (Oct 27) updates the deal.\n3/ The offer: US$0.25486 per share.\n\nThat's roughly GH₵3.00 at about GH₵11.7 per dollar (my conversion). It's also A$0.354 or £0.188.\n\nIt values the whole company at about US$210 million. That's a 26.6% premium over its Australian price the day before the deal was announced.\n\n4/ Here's the catch.\n\nThe last GSE price I found for ALLGH was GH₵5.30 (Oct 2). That's nearly 80% HIGHER than the offer in cedis.\n\nIt also barely trades. The price sat unchanged for days, on a few thousand shares at most.\n\n5/ If GSE shareholders are paid the same per share as everyone else, the payout would be about 44% below that GSE price (my calculation).\n\nCheck with your broker, and read the Scheme Booklet, to see how GSE-held shares are treated. I couldn't confirm that.\n\n6/ Even in Australia, the shares trade below the offer: A$0.265 (Oct 16) vs A$0.354. That's about 25% under.\n\nMy reading: the market isn't sure the deal will close.\n\n7/ What still has to happen:\n• Court hearing: Nov 5 \n• A shareholder vote \n• Court approval in Western Australia \n• Other regulatory approvals\n\nAustralia's foreign-investment approval is already done. The board recommends voting yes, unless a better offer appears or the independent expert disagrees.\n\n8/ Huayou has already stepped in on the project.\n\nIt took over the rights of Elevra (formerly Piedmont Lithium), now holds 22.5% of the Ghana lithium projects, and will fund US$66 million of development costs to reach 50%.\n\nSo the mine project keeps moving while the takeover is pending.\n\n9/ The money side:\n• Cash: A$7.7M, no debt \n• Spent A$6.0M this quarter \n• Cash alone covers only about 1 to 1.5 quarters (my calculation)\n\nThe company can also tap a £20M equity facility, and Huayou now pays project costs.\n\n10/ The lesson: when a takeover offer is on the table, compare it with the market price. Check how thinly the stock trades.\n\nWant to learn how to read announcements like this and invest on the GSE? Get First Steps: https://t.co/PwwFyuz7LH\n\nSource: Atlantic Lithium quarterly report, 27 Oct 2026; ALLGH price: MarketScreener; ASX price: ListCorp. \n\n⚠️ Educational only, not financial advice. You can lose money.",
